    "interview-150"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "since you can trade unlimited times, is there ever a reason to hold through a price drop?",
    "any profitable multi-day climb can be split into single-day up-moves and you'd capture the same total either way - try summing just the up-moves",
    "for each consecutive pair of days, if tomorrow's price is higher than today's, add that difference to your profit",
    "why is capturing every single positive day-to-day step always at least as good as any other trading strategy? because a longer upward run's total gain equals the sum of its daily gains, so buying at the start and selling at the end of a run gives the same profit as buying/selling on every up day within it"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "trying to find a small number of best global buy/sell windows instead of realizing every positive daily delta can just be summed directly",
    "including negative day-over-day deltas in the sum instead of skipping them",
    "assuming you need to hold across a flat or down day to catch a later rise, which is never necessary since you can re-buy the next day for free"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given a list of daily stock prices, where `prices[i]` is the price on day `i`. You may buy and sell as many times as you like, but you can hold at most one share at a time, meaning you must sell before buying again.\n\n**Return the maximum total profit achievable.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= prices.length <= 3 * 10^4",
      "0 <= prices[i] <= 10^4"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "prices strictly decreasing the whole time (answer is 0)",
      "prices strictly increasing the whole time (answer is last - first)",
      "a single price in the array",
      "prices that oscillate up and down every day"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
